WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:23.920
It’s the 1st of October, 1938.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.870
Heston airport, just outside London.

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:30.369
A man steps out of a propeller airplane to
address a crowd of journalists, officials

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:31.380
and onlookers.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:37.469
He waves in triumph a piece of paper carrying
two signatures, his and the one for a certain

00:00:37.469 --> 00:00:39.010
Mr A. Hitler.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:44.750
The man is hailed as a hero, as he announces
that he has truly achieved “peace for our

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:45.750
times”.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:50.289
This man, whose status as a hero will soon
be critiqued by none other than Winston Churchill,

00:00:50.289 --> 00:00:53.030
is the current British Prime Minister, Neville
Chamberlain.

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:57.530
He has just signed an agreement with the German
government, preventing war in Europe – at

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:03.059
least for the moment – but sacrificing the
young State of Czechoslovakia to Nazi demands.

00:01:03.059 --> 00:01:06.950
Neville Chamberlain has become synonymous
with the ill-advised policy of Appeasement,

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:13.100
an emblem of the West weakness when faced
by the aggressive bullying of the Axis powers.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:14.880
But is that fame fully deserved?

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:25.970
From Birmingham to the Bahamas
Arthur Neville Chamberlain was born in Birmingham,

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:30.659
England, on the 18th of March 1869, in a wealthy,
politically involved family, although they

00:01:30.659 --> 00:01:32.350
were not part of the aristocracy.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:36.750
His father Joseph was a Cabinet Minister during
Queen Victoria’s reign who had married twice

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:39.159
having a total of six children.

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:43.799
Beatrice and Austen, from a previous marriage,
and then Neville and three daughters, Ethel,

00:01:43.799 --> 00:01:45.999
Ida and Hilda, from his second wife.

00:01:45.999 --> 00:01:50.570
As it was tradition at that time, it was down
to the boys to perpetuate the trade and traditions

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:51.570
of the family.

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.789
Austen, Neville’s older half-brother, was
the one to initially carry out the political

00:01:54.789 --> 00:01:56.079
ambitions of the Chamberlains.

00:01:56.079 --> 00:02:00.890
He took up a string of civil service and political
posts, culminating his career as Foreign Secretary

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:01.890
in the 1920s.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:06.259
He was the mastermind behind the Treaty of
Locarno between Great Britain, France, Weimar

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:09.960
Germany, Italy and Belgium which earned him
a Nobel Peace Prize.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:14.280
Neville, on the other hand, was expected to
take the reins of the family’s many business

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:15.280
enterprises.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.190
He perfected his education first at the Rugby
School and then at Mason College, now the

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:20.700
University of Birmingham.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.500
Coming from a privileged family does not guarantee
happiness, though, and it was reported that

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.560
Neville was an introverted, shy boy, whose
school years were plagued by incidents of

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:31.790
bullying
After his formative years, aged only 21, Neville

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:36.190
was tasked by his father to manage one of
the Chamberlain’s business enterprises.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:39.420
A daunting prospect for anyone, but the task
may have been made lighter by the fact that

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:41.430
the job involved managing a plantation.

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:42.830
In the Bahamas (ref BBC)!

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:46.810
That must have been fun for young Neville,
unfortunately after some years that business

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:48.360
venture ultimately failed.

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:52.220
Neville had anyhow proven himself to be a
talented manager and supervisor.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:55.840
After returning to England he would put that
experience to successful use, first in the

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.890
private sector, and then following Austen’s
footsteps into politics.

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:07.710
Going into Public Service
In 1911 Neville Chamberlain secured his first

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:10.450
political post by winning a seat in the Birmingham
City Council.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:15.790
In the same year he married Anne Vere Cole,
with whom he would later have two children,

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:17.050
Dorothy and Francis.

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:21.260
Chamberlain’s next step was to secure a
higher position in local politics, which he

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.160
did by becoming Lord Mayor of Birmingham in
1915.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:29.120
By now aged 49, he was too old to serve in
WWI, but he did contribute to the war effort

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:35.260
as Director of the National Service, an appointment
awarded directly by Prime Minister Lloyd George.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:39.650
This job involved ‘compulsion’, which
is basically the equivalent of conscription

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:40.970
for war industries.

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:45.060
However all of Chamberlain’s attempts to
drive compulsory labour into industries vital

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:50.400
to the war effort were hampered by the unions,
by the war cabinet or Lloyd George himself.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.690
Apparently the two men could not stand each
other.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:56.710
After Chamberlain finally resigned in August
1917, Lloyd George wrote to his family

00:03:56.710 --> 00:04:00.120
“Chamberlain has resigned, and thank God
for that”

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:02.230
While Chamberlain referred to the Prime Minister
as

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:05.120
“that dirty little Welsh attorney”.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:09.100
This experience almost deterred him to stay
clear of politics, but he made a comeback

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:13.340
into public life by winning a seat in the
House of Commons in 1918 as a member of the

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:14.580
Conservative Party.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:16.310
From then on, his career was on the rise.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:21.980
In 1922 he became Postmaster General in Andrew
Bonar Law’s government, a test that he passed

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:26.120
with flying colours, becoming Minister of
Health within months and, shortly afterwards,

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:27.500
Chancellor of the Exchequer.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:32.250
All in little more than a year, and within
5 years of entering Parliament.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:35.650
By the way, for those not familiar with the
workings of the British Government, the Chancellor

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:39.791
of the Exchequer is the Secretary or Minister
in charge of the Economy.

00:04:39.791 --> 00:04:41.810
So, it is a big deal.

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:45.790
Chamberlain retained this post for the following
years, and did very well.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:49.150
In 1929 he reformed the so called “Poor
Law”, which laid down the foundations of

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:50.160
the welfare state.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.930
In the crisis that followed the Wall Street
Crash he protected the fragile state of British

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:58.080
industry by passing the Import Duties Bill,
in 1932.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:03.570
All this hard work paid off when in May 1937
Neville Chamberlain was elected leader of

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:07.360
the Conservative Party and succeeded Stanley
Baldwin as Prime Minister.

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:11.100
His early efforts were a continuation of his
welfare reforms.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:15.960
For example, the Factories Act of 1937 restricted
the number of hours that children and women

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:16.960
worked.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:21.710
And in 1938 Chamberlain supported the Holiday
with Pay Act, which gave workers a week off

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:22.720
with pay.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:27.760
By looking at his career so far you could
see how the shy boy from Rugby School had

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:32.530
turned into an active, ambitious leader willing
to bring about improvements to the society

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:35.610
around him by breaking up with established
traditions (ref BBC).

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:38.940
However, his achievements on the domestic
front were to be overshadowed by a looming

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:45.530
crisis in a faraway land, of which people
knew nothing about, to use his own words.

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:48.960
The Sudetenland Crisis
Right, I am not going to chart here the rise

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.940
of Hitler and the Nazi party and how the new
German government immediately started making

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:54.610
plans for territorial annexations.

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:57.760
I’ll actually tell you more about this later.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:03.250
So, let’s scroll our Google maps south of
Berlin instead and look at the Sudetenland

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:04.250
region instead.

00:06:04.250 --> 00:06:08.480
This is a region in then Czechoslovakia which
bordered with Germany and was home to some

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:10.650
3 Million ethnic Germans.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:16.530
Back in 1931 a Sudeten German called Konrad
Henlein had founded the Sudeten Germans People

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:20.840
Party, which advocated for the region to secede
and be annexed to Germany.

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:24.830
Needless to say, in the following decade,
Nazi leadership had openly supported Henlein’s

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:25.830
cause.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:30.750
Sure, Germans on both side of the Czechoslovak
border wanted to be reunited, but the push

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:36.080
for annexations had far more materialistic
reasons which worried the Government in Prague.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:40.010
The Sudetenland region was home to lignite
and coal mines.

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:44.230
It was also the most heavily defended, meaning
a peaceful annexation to Germany would have

00:06:44.230 --> 00:06:48.440
granted a convenient stepping stone to annex
the whole of Czechoslovakia.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:53.580
Why would Nazi Germany be so interested in
absorbing that small, young, central European

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:54.820
state?

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:56.060
Skoda.

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:59.750
These guys now manufacture affordable city
cars, but back then the Skoda works were one

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:02.540
of the largest armament manufacturers in Europe.

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:08.080
The production volume of Skoda Works between
August 1938 and September 1939 alone was nearly

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:11.950
equal to that of all British arsenal factories
in that period.

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:16.410
And sorry to jump ahead, but in 1939 a certain
Hermann Goering made a nice little profit

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:18.510
by acquiring shares in Skoda.

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:22.760
So, I am going to venture a personal observation
and say that Nazi leadership main interest

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:27.840
was not the well-being of Konrad Heinlein
and the Sudeten Germans, but rather to replenish

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.169
their arsenals and their pockets.

00:07:30.169 --> 00:07:31.639
But back to the crisis.

00:07:31.639 --> 00:07:36.610
In late summer 1938 the Nazi party asked Heinlein
to stir some trouble, as a means to create

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:38.669
an excuse for a military intervention.

00:07:38.669 --> 00:07:43.020
The government of Eduard Benes in Prague had
created a system of defensive alliance with

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:47.240
France and the Soviet Union, but neither of
them was really keen to intervene should an

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:48.300
invasion occur.

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:52.080
Chamberlain was well aware that if France
did intervene, also Britain would be dragged

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:56.640
into a European war, which not only himself,
but his whole cabinet and the majority of

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:59.260
the public opinion were keen to avoid at all
costs.

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:03.449
The British Prime Minister then took the initiative
to lead a series of negotiations throughout

00:08:03.449 --> 00:08:07.370
September 1938 to prevent an armed conflict.

00:08:07.370 --> 00:08:10.350
The first meeting with Hitler took place at
Berchtesgaden, near Munich.

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:14.770
At this meeting Hitler demanded that the Sudetenland
should be handed over to Germany.

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:19.320
Without consulting Eduard Benes, Chamberlain
agreed that those areas containing more than

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.639
50% Germans within them should be handed back
to Germany.

00:08:22.639 --> 00:08:26.270
Chamberlain managed to get the Czechs and
the French to agree to this solution.

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:30.199
On September 22nd, Chamberlain flew again
to Germany, in Bad Godesberg to meet Hitler

00:08:30.199 --> 00:08:33.110
so that the final details of the plan could
be worked out.

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:36.699
Hitler had a very low opinion of Chamberlain,
describing him in private as

00:08:36.699 --> 00:08:39.789
“Ein Arschloch”
So it was not surprise that the Fuhrer stunned

00:08:39.789 --> 00:08:43.110
the Prime Minister with a whole set of new
demands:

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:46.100
Number 1 - German troops were to occupy the
Sudetenland.

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:50.129
Number 2 - Czech lands containing a majority
of Poles and Hungarians should be returned

00:08:50.129 --> 00:08:51.649
to Poland and Hungary.

00:08:51.649 --> 00:08:55.470
Effectively, Hitler was demanding for a dissolution
of Czechoslovakia.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:59.760
This time Chamberlain and French President
Daladier rejected these demands.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:01.980
The stalemate was unblocked by Benito Mussolini.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:06.230
He proposed an emergency conference in Munich
to reach an agreement once and for all.

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:07.620
The participants were
Germany [names of countries appear on screen

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:08.620
with a ping]
Great Britain

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:10.550
France
Italy

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:12.639
Can you spot the missing guest?

00:09:12.639 --> 00:09:16.490
You are right: Czechoslovakia was not.

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:20.970
And neither was the Soviet Union, which pissed
off Joseph Stalin.

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:24.720
Without consulting the Czechs, the four powers
agreed that the Sudetenland should be given

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:26.259
to Germany immediately.

00:09:26.259 --> 00:09:29.439
The governments of Britain and France made
it clear to the Czechs that if they rejected

00:09:29.439 --> 00:09:32.769
this solution, they would have to fight Germany
by themselves.

00:09:32.769 --> 00:09:36.279
This was the crowning moment of the policy
of Appeasement.

00:09:36.279 --> 00:09:40.379
On October the 1st 1938, German troops occupied
the Sudetenland.

00:09:40.379 --> 00:09:44.320
Polish and Hungarian troops also occupied
areas of Czechoslovakia which contained a

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:46.490
majority of Poles and Magyars.

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:50.800
The dismemberment of Czechoslovakia was complete
when Germany formally annexed what is today

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:56.240
the Czech Republic in March 1939, while Slovakia
seceded and became an Axis ally.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:02.680
Let’s return now to our initial scene: Chamberlain
waving his newly signed agreement at the Heston

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:03.680
airport.

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:06.480
To him, a formal guarantee of “peace for
our time” in Europe.

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:09.119
To Hitler, just a
“scrap of paper”.

00:10:09.119 --> 00:10:11.740
Did Chamberlain take the right decision?

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:15.980
It is easy to say, with the power of hindsight
that Chamberlain’s decision was a completely

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:16.980
wrong one.

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:21.990
On a moral level, the Western powers, as members
of the League of Nations and allied of Czechoslovakia

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:24.410
had a duty to step in and protect an allied
country – or other member States – from

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:25.410
aggressions and demands worthy of a schoolyard
bully.

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:29.139
On a practical level … the matter is more
complicated, but it also weighs against Chamberlain.

00:10:29.139 --> 00:11:43.559
Now, the assumption that Britain and France
were not

00:11:43.559 --> 00:12:05.910
ready

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:45.670
for

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:49.529
a full-scale war, especially with Germany,
may have been correct.

00:12:49.529 --> 00:12:52.863
And it is understandable that Chamberlain
and Daladier were keen to avoid at all cost

00:12:52.863 --> 00:12:54.550
another carnage like the one brought about
by WWI.

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:58.119
The British Prime Minister had a particular
fear of what destruction on civilian targets

00:12:58.119 --> 00:13:01.540
could be caused by the new tactics of war
from the air.

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:05.569
It seems that upon returning from his first
flight to Munich, he observed how easy would

00:13:05.569 --> 00:13:07.819
have been for a bomber to hit at the heart
of London.

00:13:07.819 --> 00:13:11.540
One of his military advisers even told him
that two months of bombing raids would have

00:13:11.540 --> 00:13:13.249
caused one million victims.

00:13:13.249 --> 00:13:14.249
But.

00:13:14.249 --> 00:13:15.249
But!

00:13:15.249 --> 00:13:18.839
Germany itself was not yet ready for a war
with the West in the Autumn of 1938, the Wehrmacht

00:13:18.839 --> 00:13:22.959
being much weaker than it appeared from Goebbels’
propaganda reels.

00:13:22.959 --> 00:13:27.679
A full mechanization of the Heer, or land
forces, on the basis of General Guderian’s

00:13:27.679 --> 00:13:29.899
doctrine were still on the horizon.

00:13:29.899 --> 00:13:34.190
More importantly, the high echelons of the
armed forces were not a united front supporting

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:35.190
the Nazi party.

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:39.410
It is reported in fact that the Army Chief
of Staff, General Franz Halder was planning

00:13:39.410 --> 00:13:44.120
to eliminate Hitler and even carried a loaded
gun with him at all times, should the occasion

00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:45.120
arise.

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:49.310
In September and October of 1938 the German
High Command was fully aware of the country’s

00:13:49.310 --> 00:13:53.819
relative weakness and did not welcome the
prospect of a war with Czechoslovakia, even

00:13:53.819 --> 00:13:56.420
if Britain and France did not come to the
rescue.

00:13:56.420 --> 00:13:59.920
Czechoslovakia may have been a small country,
but its army was well trained, well equipped

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:02.529
and well defended by fortifications.

00:14:02.529 --> 00:14:04.269
Did Chamberlain know all this?

00:14:04.269 --> 00:14:08.489
Apparently he did receive a report from MI5
detailing Halder’s plans, but would that

00:14:08.489 --> 00:14:12.860
have been enough to change the opinion of
Chamberlain and of the majority of the Cabinet?

00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:16.829
More importantly, would that have been enough
to change the course of eight years of Foreign

00:14:16.829 --> 00:14:19.160
Policy dedicated to appeasement?

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:27.699
A brief history of Appeasement
Neville Chamberlain became synonymous with

00:14:27.699 --> 00:14:31.889
Appeaser, but certainly he wasn’t the first
political leader, neither in Great Britain

00:14:31.889 --> 00:14:36.100
nor elsewhere, to pursue a policy of appeasement
in the face of military threat and aggression.

00:14:36.100 --> 00:14:38.240
Are you ready for a brief history of appeasement?

00:14:38.240 --> 00:14:39.240
Let’s start then!

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:41.769
Round one – Japanese invasion of Manchuria.

00:14:41.769 --> 00:14:45.949
In 1931 an explosion on the South Manchuria
railway gave the Japanese military the perfect

00:14:45.949 --> 00:14:50.610
excuse to invade this Chinese region, to protect
Japanese interests in the area.

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:55.100
It was all part of a plan of aggression to
expand Tokyo’s sphere of influence, provide

00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:59.519
an outlet for the booming population and provide
new markets for a struggling industrial sector.

00:14:59.519 --> 00:15:03.249
The League of Nations’ intervention was
initially bent on economic sanctions, which

00:15:03.249 --> 00:15:07.670
Britain and France did not endorse, fearsome
of losing trade with Japan or even provoking

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:10.939
a full-scale invasion of British colonies
in the Far East.

00:15:10.939 --> 00:15:12.040
The result?

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:16.839
A weak plan to create a semi-independent state
in Manchuria, which Japan rejected by quitting

00:15:16.839 --> 00:15:21.420
the League of Nations and going on to complete
the invasion of the region and establishing

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:23.089
the puppet state of Manchukuo.

00:15:23.089 --> 00:15:24.089
Round two!

00:15:24.089 --> 00:15:25.989
– Failed Anschluss attempt, 1934.

00:15:25.989 --> 00:15:30.790
Not one year in power and Hitler and the Nazi
government were already bent on annexing Austria.

00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:34.879
Their plans kicked off with an attempted coup
carried out by Austrian Nazis, which were

00:15:34.879 --> 00:15:37.439
obviously funded and directed by Berlin.

00:15:37.439 --> 00:15:41.439
The conspirators even managed to assassinate
Prime Minister Dollfuss and the German troops

00:15:41.439 --> 00:15:42.619
were ready to move in.

00:15:42.619 --> 00:15:44.970
The Western powers, again did not intervene.

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:48.369
Who, of all people, put an end to this first
go at Anschluss?

00:15:48.369 --> 00:15:52.850
You may be surprised: Benito Mussolini, who
at this time was not yet allied to Germany

00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:55.709
and was instead tied to Vienna by a protective
treaty.

00:15:55.709 --> 00:15:59.879
Mussolini deployed two Alpine troops divisions
on the Austrian border and the still-weak

00:15:59.879 --> 00:16:01.689
Wehrmacht had to hit the brakes.

00:16:01.689 --> 00:16:02.689
Round three!

00:16:02.689 --> 00:16:04.399
– Italy invades Ethiopia, 1935.

00:16:04.399 --> 00:16:09.439
Yesterday, the defender, today the attacker:
a border incident with Ethiopia gave Mussolini

00:16:09.439 --> 00:16:13.730
the excuse to order an invasion of Ethiopia
in October 1935.

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:16.699
British public opinion was in favour to intervene
against Fascist Italy.

00:16:16.699 --> 00:16:20.410
It would have been dead easy: just shutting
down the Suez Canal to Italian shipping would

00:16:20.410 --> 00:16:21.689
have put an end to the invasion.

00:16:21.689 --> 00:16:26.839
But Prime Minister Baldwin did not want to
alienate Mussolini and his anti-German stance.

00:16:26.839 --> 00:16:29.879
Any intervention was delegated to the League
of Nations.

00:16:29.879 --> 00:16:35.769
While negotiations dragged on into 1936, the
Ethiopian capital Addis Ababa fell in May

00:16:35.769 --> 00:16:37.170
1936.

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:38.170
Round four.

00:16:38.170 --> 00:16:40.990
Hitler retakes the Rhineland, March 1936.

00:16:40.990 --> 00:16:44.819
The Rhineland includes the border areas with
the Low Countries which were meant to stay

00:16:44.819 --> 00:16:47.259
de-militarised as per the Treaty of Versailles.

00:16:47.259 --> 00:16:51.470
But taking advantage of the Ethiopian crisis,
the Germany army just up and went!

00:16:51.470 --> 00:16:53.450
They took a huge gamble, by the way.

00:16:53.450 --> 00:16:58.160
The marching units carried rifles loaded with
blanks and had strict orders to retreat immediately

00:16:58.160 --> 00:17:01.549
at the slightest sign of Franco-British resistance.

00:17:01.549 --> 00:17:08.029
But resistance did not materialise, the occupation
was carried out and Germany gained a huge

00:17:08.029 --> 00:17:11.010
advantage in preparation for the War to come.

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:12.039
Round six.

00:17:12.039 --> 00:17:15.420
Japan invades the rest of China, 1937.

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:19.540
What started as minor clashes between the
Imperial Japanese Army and the Chinese nationalist

00:17:19.540 --> 00:17:24.439
forces in July of that year quickly escalated
into a full-blown invasion.

00:17:24.439 --> 00:17:30.630
As usual, the Western powers, the Soviet Union
and the League of Nations looked on, while

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:33.760
the Japanese carried out indiscriminate bombing
of civilian targets.

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:39.000
Interestingly, help for Chiang Kai-Shek’s
Chinese nationalist army came from Nazi Germany,

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:41.139
in the shape of training and supplies.

00:17:41.139 --> 00:17:42.139
Round seven.

00:17:42.139 --> 00:17:45.330
The successful Anschluss in March 1938.

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:49.860
When Germany incorporated Austria, the West
remained silent and Italy was now on the side

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:50.860
of the invader.

00:17:50.860 --> 00:17:55.410
By now Chamberlain had replaced Baldwin as
Prime Minister, and as you may have guessed,

00:17:55.410 --> 00:18:00.440
he simply continued with the same policy put
in practice by his predecessors and fellow

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:02.570
member states in the League of Nations.

00:18:02.570 --> 00:18:06.399
In addition to the desire of avoiding a major
war on the continent, Chamberlain was also

00:18:06.399 --> 00:18:10.519
motivated by “compensating” Germany in
some way for the harsh treatment imposed by

00:18:10.519 --> 00:18:11.659
the Treaty of Versailles.

00:18:11.659 --> 00:18:16.860
Moreover, one of the key principles of the
post-WWI world order was self-determination.

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:21.340
Based on that principle, wasn’t it lawful
to allow German minorities to reunite with

00:18:21.340 --> 00:18:22.340
their motherland?

00:18:22.340 --> 00:18:24.529
And now for the …
Bonus round!

00:18:24.529 --> 00:18:28.110
– The Spanish Civil War 1936-1939.

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:32.120
We could talk for hours on end about this
war, but I’ll make just one point.

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:36.320
Italy, Germany and the Soviet Union all intervened
heavily, supporting opposing factions with

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:38.010
troops, weapons and money.

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:41.970
On the other hand, Britain and France simply
forbid the supply of arms to either side.

00:18:41.970 --> 00:18:47.059
This non-interventionist policy was a continuation
of Baldwin’s plan not to alienate Mussolini’s

00:18:47.059 --> 00:18:48.059
sympathies.

00:18:48.059 --> 00:18:56.880
Yeah, it worked a treat (!).
From Appeasement to World War II

00:18:56.880 --> 00:19:02.070
And now let’s zoom back to London, and fast
forward to the 3rd of September 1939.

00:19:02.070 --> 00:19:05.572
Neville Chamberlain has just announced on
the radio that Great Britain is now at war

00:19:05.572 --> 00:19:06.572
with Germany.

00:19:06.572 --> 00:19:10.460
All his efforts to maintain peace in Europe,
were all vain in the end.

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:13.470
Chamberlain’s leadership during the war
is generally remembered as lacklustre at best

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:17.010
and lacking in initiative, being these the
months of the so called ‘Phoney War’,

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:20.769
in which French and British troops did little
but stand by the Maginot line, waiting for

00:19:20.769 --> 00:19:21.800
a German attack.

00:19:21.800 --> 00:19:26.570
However it is fair to recognise one important
victory which can be attributed to Chamberlain’s

00:19:26.570 --> 00:19:31.520
foresight: the Battle of Britain, or the months
long duel in which the Royal Air Force established

00:19:31.520 --> 00:19:36.750
air superiority over the Luftwaffe, thus preventing
Operation Sea Lion, the invasion of Great

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:37.750
Britain.

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:41.420
In the months after the Munich Treaty, Chamberlain
led the development of Britain’s air defence

00:19:41.420 --> 00:19:45.299
systems, the more sophisticated in Europe:
it was thanks to him that the Royal Air Force

00:19:45.299 --> 00:19:49.700
could rely on the revolutionary invention
of RADAR to coordinate wave after wave of

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:52.500
Spitfire fighter planes against incoming German
squadrons.

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:56.010
But Neville Chamberlain could not reap the
reward for such a success.

00:19:56.010 --> 00:20:01.230
On the 10th of May 1940, after a failed attempt
to reclaim Norway from Nazi occupation, Chamberlain

00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:02.230
resigned.

00:20:02.230 --> 00:20:05.570
Winston Churchill – the main architect of
the Norwegian debacle – became the new Prime

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:06.570
Minister.

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:10.309
Chamberlain continue to serve in Churchill’s
cabinet, but resigned for good in October

00:20:10.309 --> 00:20:12.990
1940, too ill with late stage bowel cancer.

00:20:12.990 --> 00:20:16.909
Not one month later, on the 9th of November,
Chamberlain died in his estate near Reading,

00:20:16.909 --> 00:20:17.909
in southern England.

00:20:17.909 --> 00:20:21.660
Final words
As you have heard, it is too easy to dismiss

00:20:21.660 --> 00:20:26.440
Neville Chamberlain as gullible and naïve
appeaser, who did not respond to Hitler’s

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:28.309
provocations for all the wrong reasons.

00:20:28.309 --> 00:20:32.700
The reality was far more complex and it is
understandable how Appeasement may have been

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:35.190
considered as the best option at that time.

00:20:35.190 --> 00:20:39.840
However, Chamberlain had a record for breaking
away with established policies and had he

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:44.169
listened to other opinions rather than the
Conservative majority, he could have changed

00:20:44.169 --> 00:20:46.590
the course of history for the better.

00:20:46.590 --> 00:20:50.470
Winston Churchill probably best summarised
the Czech crisis and Appeasement with one

00:20:50.470 --> 00:20:55.570
of his most quotable lines, addressed to Chamberlain:
“You were given the choice between war and

00:20:55.570 --> 00:20:56.570
dishonour.

00:20:56.570 --> 00:21:20.789
You chose dishonour, and you will have war”

